.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt VGPU-SHM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vgpu-shm
.Nd run virtio-gpu over a region shared with a virtual machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/vgpu-shm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The vgpu-shm character device, major 10 and minor 243, gives the kernel a
memory region and the doorbells of a virtual machine that runs a
virtio-gpu device in it, and reports what the kernel's driver does there.
The kernel then binds
.Xr virtio-gpu 4
over the region, whose layout and rules
.Pa rach2/sys/virtio/vgpushm.h
defines: a control page, the rings of two virtqueues and a heap from which
the driver takes its commands and every resource's backing.
The device's DMA addresses are offsets in the region, and the kernel
checks everything the device writes there before using it.
The device has mode 0600, and every call needs a privileged caller.
.Xr ivshmemd 8
is the program that makes the region and serves it to QEMU's
.Sy ivshmem-doorbell .
.Pp
The ioctls:
.Bl -tag -width VGS_IOC_ATTACH
.It Dv VGS_IOC_ATTACH
Read a
.Vt struct vgs_attach :
.Ar memfd
is a memfd or other tmpfs file sealed with
.Dv F_SEAL_SHRINK ,
whose size is a power of two from 32 KiB to 64 MiB;
.Ar bell_in
are two eventfds the device signals for the driver, the queues' vector 0
and the control vector 1, and
.Ar bell_out
two the driver signals for the device, none of them one of
.Ar bell_in ;
.Ar peer
is the driver's ivshmem peer id, below 65536.
With every eventfd -1 the driver polls the region instead.
.Ar flags
and
.Ar reserved
are 0.
The kernel holds the region's pages and the eventfds until the detach, and
binds virtio-gpu once the device offers a running epoch; a device that
leaves the epoch, fails or resets is dropped and bound again in a new one.
One region is attached at a time.
.It Dv VGS_IOC_DETACH
Read a
.Vt uint32_t :
.Dv VGS_DETACH_ORDERLY
tells a device that is still there that the driver has gone, and
.Dv VGS_DETACH_LOST
says that the device's machine has died.
Either way the driver unplugs the display, fails every pending fence with
.Er ENODEV ,
frees every resource and gives the region back; the call returns once it
has.
A buffer whose exported dma-buf still exists at the detach keeps its pages
of the heap until it is freed, and a region attached meanwhile over the same
memory leaves them out of its heap until then.
Only the descriptor that attached may detach.
Its last close detaches as
.Dv VGS_DETACH_LOST
does.
.It Dv VGS_IOC_INFO
Fill a
.Vt struct vgs_info :
whether a region is attached and virtio-gpu bound over it, the epoch, the
last states of the driver and the device and the device's error, whether
both sides use the doorbells, the times virtio-gpu was bound, the
doorbells rung each way by vector, the passes that found the device's work
before its doorbell, the heap's pages, those in use and the
allocations it refused, and the blobs mapped from the VM's pages since the
attach, the pages the live maps hold, the live maps, and those taken back.
.El
.Pp
On x86-64, with the doorbells, the driver accepts page lists: a mappable
blob of the device's own memory, such as Venus's host-visible memory, is
answered with the device machine's physical pages, which KVM resolves
through the machine's memory slots into the frames of its QEMU and holds,
and a program's
.Xr mmap 2
of the blob maps those frames with the cache type the device gave.
When the machine's memory behind them changes, a slot over them moves or
goes, or the machine dies, the frames are taken back: every mapping of the
blob is removed, and the next access raises
.Dv SIGBUS ,
as an access to a revoked mapping does on Linux.
.Pp
With a KVM guest's ivshmem-doorbell, the device's doorbells reach the
kernel through KVM ioeventfds and the driver's raise the guest's MSI-X
vectors through irqfds, with no exit to QEMU;
.Xr proc 5
counts both in
.Pa /proc/stat .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller is not privileged.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
A region is already attached.
.It Bq Er EBADF
The region's descriptor does not name a tmpfs file, or an eventfd's
descriptor is not an eventfd.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The region's size or seals are wrong, some but not all eventfds are given,
one eventfd is given both as a doorbell the device rings and as one it is
rung by, the peer is too large, a flag or reserved field is nonzero, or the
detach's argument is unknown.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
The descriptor did not attach the region, or none is attached.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel cannot map the region or allocate its records.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
An argument cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
The request is unknown.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr virtio-gpu 4 ,
.Xr ivshmemd 8
